CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 4 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
The read-implication form, on by default, states each read's round selection as one implication per
round instead of a mux nested `rounds` deep. The two forms must agree, and this
is the direction that can actually catch a mistake: dropping or weakening a
read's constraint leaves the read free, the solver invents a value, and a safe
program reports a violation it does not have.

Runs with no flag on purpose: this is the default path.

Verified discriminating by mutation: with the per-round loop made to emit
nothing, this test reports VERIFICATION FAILED while the default encoding still
reports SUCCESSFUL.

The reader thread runs concurrently on purpose. A read placed after
pthread_join is not shared, so it never reaches the read-from encoding and the
test would pass whatever that encoding did -- measured, on an earlier version
of this program that did exactly that.
